R=[]
R.append(scoped_record('c0849','four-bee-quasi-robot-provision',"Biting the Sun — Four-BEE’s quasi-robot provision", "Don't Bite the Sun / Drinking Sapphire Wine",'The domed city before the narrator’s desert exile',['book'],
 'Tanith Lee’s linked novels','Ordinary city life before the protagonist’s exile','Human residents of Four-BEE',
 ['Desert exiles and their later handmade settlement','Other domes as additional copies of the same material model','Resurrection as a separate society'],
 'Four-BEE’s quasi-robots supply daily needs, repair damage and replace human bodies. Residents can obtain goods without earning ordinary wages; payment involves expressing gratitude rather than performing productive labor. This gives city residents a strong material floor, although dependence on the city and the possibility of punitive exile make a perfectly unconditional guarantee too strong. Body replacement reinforces support but is not the sole reason this qualifies.\n\nThe same quasi-robots run the governing Committee, putting effective productive authority near the autonomous-machine end of control. Humans can request services without owning the system. The narrator repeatedly fails to find useful employment because machines already perform the tasks. A high nonworking estimate is therefore defensible as a broad editorial reading, with a wide interval allowing for meaningful personal art, study, care or exploration. Those activities still count as work when useful. The desert community is outside this point, and the other domes are not counted again merely because they have different names.',
 [-.8,-.95,.9],[[-1,-.4],[-1,-.75],[.6,.99]],
 ['Robots actually provide daily goods, repair and bodily support without productive wage requirements; city membership and exile qualify the guarantee.','Quasi-robots themselves govern through the Committee; requests and consumer choice do not establish human ownership.','The unsuccessful search for useful work and machine performance of routine tasks support a high editorial estimate, not a census; personal useful activity remains possible.'],[P+'sunbite'],5))
R.append(scoped_record('c0851','red-star-martian-commonwealth','Red Star — The Martian commonwealth','Red Star','Voluntary labor with automated production and free consumption',['book'],
 'Bogdanov’s novel','The established Martian society visited by Leonid','Organic Martians, explicitly used as a humanlike population comparison',
 ['Earth’s contemporary revolutionary economy','Actual Soviet economic history','Machines as people in the labor denominator'],
 'Bogdanov’s Mars combines automated factories with freely available consumption. Production and labor needs are tracked through a statistical coordination system, while people choose unpaid work and can change occupations. Access to ordinary goods is therefore not a wage contract, and common living standards support a favorable guarantee estimate. This is an actual technological and distributive arrangement rather than a communist label alone. Resource pressures and the reference’s limited account of every necessity keep the estimate provisional.\n\nThe system is presented as a common productive order coordinated through information, so control is placed toward shared organic-person control, with a broad interval for the central institute’s practical authority. Statistical machinery is not assumed to be a sovereign AI. The population is Martian and is disclosed as an analogy to the human axes. Most inhabitants reportedly choose useful work despite being free not to do it. Voluntary factory supervision, medicine and science remain work; short days and unpaid participation cannot supply an all-age nonworking percentage, which remains unresolved.',
 [-.85,.7,None],[[-1,-.45],[.2,1],None],
 ['Accessible editorial material explicitly links automatic production, free consumption and voluntary unpaid labor; common housing corroborates ordinary rather than solely elite access.','Communal production and flexible voluntary participation suggest distributed control; the central statistical institute creates uncertainty, not evidence of autonomous AI sovereignty.','Most Martians reportedly work voluntarily; no all-age activity census permits a percentage. Martians are an explicit organic-person proxy.'],[P+'redstar',P+'redstar-summary'],2,proxy=True))
R.append(scoped_record('c0855','nosov-sun-city-needs-machinery','Dunno — Sun City’s machine-supported access','Dunno in Sun City / Dunno on the Moon','Technological city with need-based goods and continuing design work',['book'],
 'Nosov’s book trilogy; Sun City economic reference corroborated in the next book’s opening','The functioning city’s established provision, before Dunno’s temporary magical disruption','Sun City’s organic miniature people, explicitly treated as a humanlike population proxy',
 ['Dunno’s magic wand as productive infrastructure','Moon society as the same economy','Flower City’s later adoption as another point'],
 'Sun City’s technological economy is distinguished from the visitor’s magic wand. An opening passage in the next novel describes machines taking over clothing manufacture and goods being taken according to need, explicitly comparing the arrangement with Sun City. The city therefore has a concrete productive and allocation mechanism, rather than qualifying just because it is cheerful or futuristic. Guarantees are provisionally favorable, with uncertainty because the short passage does not establish a complete housing and healthcare charter.\n\nThe available references do not explain final ownership of the whole productive system clearly enough to score control. A shared-access interpretation is provisional and does not turn factories or civic coordination into sovereign AI. These inhabitants are miniature organic persons, disclosed as a comparison to humans. Designers still invent clothing styles and respond to preferences, while scientists and other residents pursue useful activities. Machines doing the sewing do not establish that everyone stops working. Nonworking stays unresolved, and neither Flower City’s later imitation nor magical disruptions create additional retained societies.',
 [-.65,None,None],[[-.95,0],None,None],
 ['A bounded indexed primary opening links labor-saving clothing machines, need-based access and Sun City’s existing arrangement; the reference separates technology from the visitor’s magic.','Final ownership and authority over city production are not established; communal-access regime remains provisional.','Design, research and other useful contributions continue; miniature organic inhabitants are an explicit proxy and no activity census is available.'],[P+'sun-city',P+'sun-factories'],2,proxy=True))
D=[]
for cid,key,reason,analysis in [
 ('c0850','marrow','A luxury Great Ship does not yet establish the passengers’ material production and access arrangement.','Adjustable environments, long voyages and an elite captain are insufficient to infer automated necessities or lifetime entitlements.'),
 ('c0852','andromeda','The short reference establishes communist futurism but not the required material mechanism.','Active scientists and advanced spacecraft do not show how ordinary residents obtain necessities independently of work. Skip further reading in this pass.'),
 ('c0853','bull','Tormance’s oppression and selective lifespan limits do not establish a zero variant.','A human elite’s cruelty and technological inequality do not prove actual restraint of labor-replacing capacity.'),
 ('c0854','circle','Affluence and engineered entertainment do not establish productive automation in the bounded summary.','The city’s ordinary production and material access remain unclear; no full novel investigation.'),
 ('c0856','moon','Resistance to giant crops is not enough to establish deliberate retention of human productive work.','The lunar novel confirms wage dependence and monopolist obstruction, but the short source does not connect that obstruction to automation replacing labor. Do not inflate the zero category.'),
 ('c0857','geometers','The Geometers’ advanced coercive order lacks a clear material access mechanism in the short reference.','Technological strength, social conformity and human ancestry do not alone establish labor-independent provision.'),
 ('c0858','arank','Arank’s affluence is described, but the accessible references do not clearly link it to automatic production.','The reader review is a useful lead, not enough to reconstruct ownership and provision. No novel-length rescue.'),
 ('c0859','guest','An android archivist and free-looking futuristic transport do not establish Moscow’s household economy.','The verified title is a TV adaptation of One Hundred Years Ahead; no inference that all books and adaptations depict identical guarantees.'),
 ('c0860','4338','Future transport and communications do not establish a technological necessities floor.','Bounded indexed passages describe useful scholarly effort and futuristic services; the missing English page was not treated as evidence and the novella was not read to rescue the lead.')]:
 D.append(short_disposition(cid,'needs_evidence',reason,[P+key],analysis))
